{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Also to Noadiah the prophetess</b> — The consensus of the commentators is that this is Shemaiah son of Delaiah, and he was called thus because he said נועד אל בית האלהים ('let us meet at the house of Elohim'). And the word 'the prophetess' — it comes on account of the form of the word נועדיה, which is feminine in form, not on account of the matter — like saying 'said Kohelet' [where the feminine form does not indicate a woman]. For to say that Noadiah was a woman who prophesied falsely against him is the far-fetched interpretation, and all the arguments turn aside." "<b>And the word 'also to Noadiah'</b> — to include [her/him] along with Shemaiah. And this is the correct interpretation."], :book :nehemiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra rejects the plain sense that Noadiah was a female false prophet; the grammar is compressed and the last clause is elliptical.", 1 "The feminine pronoun in 'along with Shemaiah' does not arise since Ibn Ezra holds Noadiah is male; rendered neutrally."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Nehemiah -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/nechemya"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:15:17.839064811Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}